,\\frac{1}{1},\\,\\frac{6}{3}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨2,\\,1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding unit direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{∇f(1,1,3)}{‖∇f(1,1,3)‖} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(2)^{2}}}⟨2,\\,1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the unit direction of $⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩.$" 13194139535618 17592186045443 true] [17592186523222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523227 13194139535618 17592186045443 true] [17592186523222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535618 17592186045443 true] [17592186523222 1071 "In what unit direction does the function $f(x,y,z)=\\ln (x^{6}y^{4}z^{4})$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2,2,2)$?" 13194139535618 17592186045443 true] [17592186523222 1072 #uuid "aaff19d2-1089-5ffb-bb3e-92011188d5ba" 13194139535618 17592186045443 true] [17592186523222 1074 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (x^{6}y^{4}z^{4})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln (x^{6}y^{4}z^{4})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\ln (x^{6}y^{4}z^{4}))⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{1}{x^{6}y^{4}z^{4}}\\cdot 6x^{5}y^{4}z^{4},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{6}y^{4}z^{4}}\\cdot 4x^{6}y^{3}z^{4},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{6}y^{4}z^{4}}\\cdot 4x^{6}y^{4}z^{3}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{6}{x},\\,\\frac{4}{y},\\,\\frac{4}{z}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(2,2,2) & =⟨\\frac{6}{2},\\,\\frac{4}{2},\\,\\frac{4}{2}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨3,\\,2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding unit direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =-\\frac{∇f(2,2,2)}{‖∇f(2,2,2)‖} \\\\ & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(2)^{2}}}⟨3,\\,2,\\,2⟩ \\\\ & =⟨-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the unit direction of $⟨-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt